WEBVTT

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:20.020
Authentication is not authorization. Imagine an Aster employee
signs in correctly, then changes an invoice identifier and
receives a Birch customer's invoice. The intended policy forbids
that cross-tenant access, and this user has no support-role
exception.

00:00:22.094 --> 00:00:33.814
The problem is broken object-level authorization, often called
BOLA. The application knows who the user is, but failed to check
whether that user may read this particular object.

00:00:35.889 --> 00:00:48.689
A safe query can return a forbidden row. Parameterized SQL keeps
data values separate from query structure, helping prevent SQL
injection. It does not decide whether the caller is entitled to
the selected invoice.

00:00:50.763 --> 00:01:07.723
Enforce the policy on the server. Derive identity and tenant
context from trusted server-side state, and check the requested
action against the object's ownership, tenant, and applicable
access policy. Do not trust a client-supplied tenant identifier
without validation.

00:01:09.797 --> 00:01:18.157
Replacing sequential identifiers with UUIDs does not repair a
missing permission check. Hiding a button does not enforce the
backend policy either.

00:01:20.231 --> 00:01:34.511
Test both allowed and denied access: permitted own-tenant
invoices, forbidden cross-tenant invoices, and restricted objects
within the same tenant. Test privileged support access separately
if that role exists.

00:01:36.586 --> 00:01:44.426
In an interview, name the broken boundary, propose a server-side
fix, and describe the regression test that would catch the failure
again.
